PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to favor E as the answer that best tracks the actual language of the Ontario Rules (Rule 3.3-3). The facts give James an objectively reasonable belief in an imminent same-night risk to an identifiable person, and the information he passed on—names, current Ontario addresses, and the children’s identities—directly enabled police to locate Eric or his ex-wife before the stated plan could unfold.  

Colleagues 4 and 10 raise a fair point about the “minimum necessary” limit, but I’m not persuaded the Florida addresses or children’s identities actually breach it. Police routinely ask for any known properties and family details when someone is believed to be at immediate risk of flight or self-harm; even if those details turned out to be extraneous in this instance, the rule is framed in terms of what the lawyer reasonably believed was needed at the time, not with perfect hindsight. The core disclosure was still proportionate and aimed squarely at prevention.  

If the group remains split, it might help to test whether the Florida addresses are truly disqualifying or simply surplus that does not void an otherwise permissible disclosure. That distinction seems decisive for choosing between C and E.}

